1
00:00:02,950 --> 00:00:06,551
In 2007, Nokia was almost untouchable.

2
00:00:06,911 --> 00:00:12,420
It sold approximately 437 million
mobile devices in a single year.

3
00:00:12,856 --> 00:00:17,836
Its estimated share of the global mobile
phone market was thirty-eight percent.

4
00:00:18,382 --> 00:00:24,429
Its phones were everywhere. In offices,
schools, taxis, airports, and millions of

5
00:00:24,469 --> 00:00:25,454
homes.

6
00:00:25,494 --> 00:00:29,107
Nokia wasn't merely participating
in the mobile revolution.

7
00:00:29,665 --> 00:00:31,060
It was leading it.

8
00:00:31,391 --> 00:00:35,990
And yet, just six years later, the company
would announce the sale of its legendary

9
00:00:36,030 --> 00:00:37,420
handset business.

10
00:00:38,013 --> 00:00:41,916
How does a market leader lose
the market it helped create?

11
00:00:43,213 --> 00:00:45,248
September third, 2013.

12
00:00:45,821 --> 00:00:50,894
Nokia announced an agreement to sell
substantially all of its Devices and Services

13
00:00:50,934 --> 00:00:52,304
business to Microsoft.

14
00:00:52,997 --> 00:00:57,804
The announced transaction was valued at
approximately five point four four billion

15
00:00:57,844 --> 00:01:01,094
euros, including a patent
licensing component.

16
00:01:01,577 --> 00:01:05,923
For millions of customers, Nokia had
been synonymous with the mobile phone.

17
00:01:06,354 --> 00:01:11,148
Now, the company that once defined the
category was preparing to leave handset

18
00:01:11,188 --> 00:01:12,758
manufacturing behind.

19
00:01:13,345 --> 00:01:15,326
But this wasn't the bankruptcy of Nokia.

20
00:01:15,892 --> 00:01:17,870
The company would continue operating.

21
00:01:18,447 --> 00:01:22,609
What had collapsed was its position as
a dominant mobile phone manufacturer.

22
00:01:24,501 --> 00:01:27,693
Nokia's mobile dominance
didn't happen by accident.

23
00:01:28,227 --> 00:01:32,536
The Finnish company built an extraordinary
combination of hardware engineering,

24
00:01:33,026 --> 00:01:36,676
manufacturing scale, distribution,
and brand recognition.

25
00:01:37,301 --> 00:01:39,320
Its devices were dependable.

26
00:01:39,681 --> 00:01:41,386
Its designs were familiar.

27
00:01:41,899 --> 00:01:46,058
Its supply chain reached markets
that competitors struggled to serve.

28
00:01:46,537 --> 00:01:51,127
By the late nineteen-nineties, Nokia had
become the world's leading mobile phone

29
00:01:51,167 --> 00:01:52,167
manufacturer.

30
00:01:52,619 --> 00:01:55,722
And it continued expanding
through the next decade.

31
00:01:56,174 --> 00:02:01,384
Its greatest advantage was the ability
to make mobile technology accessible to

32
00:02:01,424 --> 00:02:03,354
enormous numbers of people.

33
00:02:05,046 --> 00:02:08,010
There's a popular explanation
for Nokia's decline.

34
00:02:08,623 --> 00:02:11,440
That it failed because it
didn't understand smartphones.

35
00:02:12,362 --> 00:02:13,502
But that isn't accurate.

36
00:02:14,094 --> 00:02:19,008
Nokia was already selling sophisticated
mobile computers before the first iPhone

37
00:02:19,048 --> 00:02:20,048
appeared.

38
00:02:20,428 --> 00:02:26,296
Its Nseries and Eseries devices combined
cameras, internet access, email,

39
00:02:26,686 --> 00:02:30,256
navigation, and multimedia capabilities.

40
00:02:30,986 --> 00:02:36,377
The Nokia N95, introduced in
2007, was a powerful example.

41
00:02:36,843 --> 00:02:40,128
Nokia understood that phones
were becoming computers.

42
00:02:40,738 --> 00:02:46,254
What it underestimated was how radically
the experience of using those computers was

43
00:02:46,294 --> 00:02:47,384
about to change.

44
00:02:49,217 --> 00:02:53,825
On January ninth, 2007,
Apple introduced the iPhone.

45
00:02:54,402 --> 00:02:55,849
It wasn't the first smartphone.

46
00:02:56,562 --> 00:02:59,015
And it wasn't the first touchscreen phone.

47
00:02:59,475 --> 00:03:01,826
But it brought a different
philosophy into focus.

48
00:03:02,359 --> 00:03:03,899
A large touchscreen.

49
00:03:04,374 --> 00:03:07,942
Software designed around
direct interaction.

50
00:03:08,430 --> 00:03:12,972
And a product experience in which hardware
and operating system were tightly

51
00:03:13,012 --> 00:03:14,012
integrated.

52
00:03:14,344 --> 00:03:18,837
For Nokia, the immediate threat was
not simply another expensive handset.

53
00:03:19,504 --> 00:03:23,196
It was a different definition of
what a smartphone should feel like.

54
00:03:24,829 --> 00:03:28,109
The competition soon expanded
beyond individual devices.

55
00:03:28,878 --> 00:03:31,781
Apple launched its App Store in 2008.

56
00:03:32,140 --> 00:03:36,660
Google's Android ecosystem began
growing across multiple manufacturers.

57
00:03:37,232 --> 00:03:42,254
Applications, developers, operating
systems, and services became increasingly

58
00:03:42,294 --> 00:03:43,294
important.

59
00:03:43,760 --> 00:03:49,267
A smartphone was no longer judged only by
its camera, battery, or physical design.

60
00:03:49,845 --> 00:03:54,089
Its software ecosystem determined
what users could do with it.

61
00:03:54,432 --> 00:03:59,496
Nokia had services, applications,
and an established operating system.

62
00:03:59,850 --> 00:04:04,501
But the market was moving toward integrated
platforms at extraordinary speed.

63
00:04:06,313 --> 00:04:10,292
Nokia's principal smartphone
operating system was Symbian.

64
00:04:10,716 --> 00:04:13,831
It had helped power millions
of capable devices.

65
00:04:14,636 --> 00:04:20,450
But its legacy architecture and development
complexity made competing in the new

66
00:04:20,490 --> 00:04:22,800
touchscreen-centered market difficult.

67
00:04:23,359 --> 00:04:28,454
Nokia continued investing in Symbian while
pursuing alternative software strategies.

68
00:04:29,027 --> 00:04:31,986
The challenge wasn't a complete
absence of innovation.

69
00:04:32,640 --> 00:04:37,851
It was the difficulty of coordinating that
innovation into one competitive platform.

70
00:04:38,278 --> 00:04:44,188
Every delay gave Apple and Android more
time to attract customers, developers, and

71
00:04:44,228 --> 00:04:45,228
manufacturers.

72
00:04:47,117 --> 00:04:50,263
Nokia explored several
routes into the future.

73
00:04:50,596 --> 00:04:52,633
Symbian remained important.

74
00:04:53,210 --> 00:04:57,908
MeeGo represented another attempt to
build a modern smartphone platform.

75
00:04:58,361 --> 00:05:03,489
The company also invested in services,
mapping, and new user experiences.

76
00:05:04,118 --> 00:05:08,975
These efforts showed that Nokia
recognized the need for change.

77
00:05:09,576 --> 00:05:14,757
But recognizing change and delivering a
unified response are different problems.

78
00:05:15,311 --> 00:05:20,027
Multiple technologies required resources,
management attention, and difficult

79
00:05:20,067 --> 00:05:21,857
prioritization decisions.

80
00:05:22,498 --> 00:05:25,501
Meanwhile, the market was
becoming less forgiving.

81
00:05:26,042 --> 00:05:30,477
The longer Nokia's transition took,
the harder catching up became.

82
00:05:32,318 --> 00:05:38,055
In September 2010, Stephen Elop became
Nokia's president and chief executive.

83
00:05:38,633 --> 00:05:43,407
He inherited a company with enormous assets
and a rapidly deteriorating competitive

84
00:05:43,447 --> 00:05:45,837
position in high-end smartphones.

85
00:05:46,473 --> 00:05:50,339
The question was no longer
whether Nokia needed to change.

86
00:05:50,905 --> 00:05:51,905
It was how.

87
00:05:52,068 --> 00:05:55,952
Should Nokia continue developing
its own smartphone platforms?

88
00:05:56,669 --> 00:05:58,376
Should it embrace Android?

89
00:05:58,908 --> 00:06:01,481
Or should it pursue a
different partnership?

90
00:06:01,955 --> 00:06:03,798
Each option carried risks.

91
00:06:04,353 --> 00:06:08,638
But one decision would fundamentally
redirect the company's future.

92
00:06:10,345 --> 00:06:16,573
On February eleventh, 2011, Nokia and
Microsoft announced a strategic partnership.

93
00:06:17,116 --> 00:06:21,382
Windows Phone would become Nokia's
primary smartphone platform.

94
00:06:21,931 --> 00:06:23,566
The logic was ambitious.

95
00:06:24,307 --> 00:06:29,101
Nokia would contribute hardware,
distribution, and design expertise.

96
00:06:29,797 --> 00:06:35,319
Microsoft would contribute software,
services, and a broader technology ecosystem.

97
00:06:36,005 --> 00:06:41,268
Together, they hoped to establish a
powerful alternative to Apple and Android.

98
00:06:41,864 --> 00:06:46,767
But Nokia was committing its smartphone
future to a platform that had not yet

99
00:06:46,807 --> 00:06:49,077
established comparable market scale.

100
00:06:49,534 --> 00:06:51,520
It was a strategic gamble.

101
00:06:53,331 --> 00:06:56,142
The partnership created
an immediate problem.

102
00:06:56,586 --> 00:07:01,747
Nokia needed customers to continue buying
its existing smartphones while waiting for

103
00:07:01,787 --> 00:07:03,477
its new Windows Phone products.

104
00:07:04,093 --> 00:07:08,102
But the announcement made the
company's future direction clear.

105
00:07:08,604 --> 00:07:11,753
Symbian was no longer the
long-term centerpiece.

106
00:07:12,556 --> 00:07:17,756
In Nokia's own annual reporting, the company
acknowledged that demand for Symbian

107
00:07:17,796 --> 00:07:21,906
devices deteriorated faster than
anticipated during the transition.

108
00:07:22,521 --> 00:07:25,683
The new products needed time
to establish themselves.

109
00:07:26,447 --> 00:07:28,694
The old products were losing momentum.

110
00:07:29,275 --> 00:07:33,162
Nokia was caught between two
generations of its own strategy.

111
00:07:35,037 --> 00:07:41,497
In October 2011, Nokia introduced its first
Lumia smartphones running Windows Phone.

112
00:07:42,050 --> 00:07:47,389
The Lumia family would showcase distinctive
industrial design, imaging capabilities,

113
00:07:48,019 --> 00:07:50,169
and Microsoft's mobile software.

114
00:07:50,790 --> 00:07:53,395
These weren't simply poorly made phones.

115
00:07:54,031 --> 00:07:57,647
Nokia continued demonstrating
serious hardware expertise.

116
00:07:58,269 --> 00:08:00,500
But strong hardware wasn't enough.

117
00:08:01,011 --> 00:08:06,337
Apple and Android already had powerful
ecosystems of users, developers,

118
00:08:07,067 --> 00:08:09,017
applications, and commercial partners.

119
00:08:09,604 --> 00:08:13,491
Nokia now needed to persuade
people to join a third platform.

120
00:08:14,179 --> 00:08:17,315
And platform competition rewards momentum.

121
00:08:19,197 --> 00:08:22,552
Nokia's own financial
reporting revealed the damage.

122
00:08:23,115 --> 00:08:28,252
Its smartphone business faced declining
Symbian demand, pressure on selling prices,

123
00:08:28,292 --> 00:08:31,322
and difficulties during
the platform transition.

124
00:08:31,843 --> 00:08:34,392
The company was still
selling millions of phones.

125
00:08:34,945 --> 00:08:38,318
But scale alone no longer
guaranteed leadership.

126
00:08:38,918 --> 00:08:42,128
Customers were moving toward
competing smartphone ecosystems.

127
00:08:42,958 --> 00:08:47,291
Nokia's challenge was not merely producing
an attractive replacement handset.

128
00:08:47,788 --> 00:08:52,024
It needed to rebuild its position in
a market whose software and developer

129
00:08:52,064 --> 00:08:55,054
relationships were already
shifting elsewhere.

130
00:08:55,459 --> 00:08:58,048
Time had become its scarcest resource.

131
00:08:59,878 --> 00:09:05,720
By September 2013, Nokia and Microsoft had
agreed on the sale of substantially all

132
00:09:05,760 --> 00:09:08,410
of Nokia's Devices and Services business.

133
00:09:08,958 --> 00:09:14,698
The announced transaction price was
approximately five point four four billion

134
00:09:14,908 --> 00:09:17,118
euros, including
patent-related arrangements.

135
00:09:17,747 --> 00:09:21,592
On April twenty-fifth, 2014,
the sale was completed.

136
00:09:22,151 --> 00:09:26,187
Nokia retained important businesses
and intellectual property.

137
00:09:26,708 --> 00:09:30,651
It would continue as a major
telecommunications technology company.

138
00:09:31,127 --> 00:09:35,707
But the era of Nokia as the manufacturer
behind one of the world's most recognizable

139
00:09:35,747 --> 00:09:38,257
handset empires had ended.

140
00:09:39,948 --> 00:09:42,750
So what destroyed Nokia's
handset dominance?

141
00:09:43,343 --> 00:09:44,648
Not one phone.

142
00:09:45,144 --> 00:09:46,821
Not one competitor.

143
00:09:47,405 --> 00:09:49,828
And not one executive decision.

144
00:09:50,352 --> 00:09:53,517
The evidence points to an
accumulation of problems.

145
00:09:54,095 --> 00:09:56,042
Legacy software complexity.

146
00:09:56,633 --> 00:10:00,392
Slow execution during a
fundamental market transition.

147
00:10:01,107 --> 00:10:03,078
Fragmented platform development.

148
00:10:03,789 --> 00:10:09,098
And a late strategic commitment to an
ecosystem that struggled to match its rivals.

149
00:10:09,625 --> 00:10:12,170
Nokia's hardware strengths remained real.

150
00:10:12,599 --> 00:10:15,504
But the industry's competitive
advantage had moved.

151
00:10:16,043 --> 00:10:18,745
The company could still
build impressive phones.

152
00:10:19,379 --> 00:10:22,490
It could no longer reliably
win the platform battle.

153
00:10:24,211 --> 00:10:27,723
Nokia's story contains a warning
for every market leader.

154
00:10:28,216 --> 00:10:30,822
Dominance is evidence of past success.

155
00:10:31,507 --> 00:10:33,594
It is not proof of future relevance.

156
00:10:34,336 --> 00:10:38,000
The greatest danger isn't always
failing to recognize change.

157
00:10:38,639 --> 00:10:44,418
Sometimes it's recognizing change, investing
in it, and still moving too slowly to

158
00:10:44,458 --> 00:10:47,188
turn that investment
into a winning strategy.

159
00:10:47,595 --> 00:10:49,267
Nokia didn't disappear.

160
00:10:49,877 --> 00:10:52,462
It reinvented itself beyond handsets.

161
00:10:52,984 --> 00:10:58,286
But its mobile phone empire became a case
study in what happens when a company's

162
00:10:58,326 --> 00:11:03,536
strongest capabilities no longer match
the market's most important demands.

163
00:11:04,218 --> 00:11:06,110
Every brand leaves evidence.

